\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Keeps Ukraine 'B-/B' Ratings On CreditWatch Negative\b0\par\par\uc2 2,516 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par 22:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 11 Mar 2022 16:07 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Keeps Ukraine 'B-/B' Ratings On CreditWatch Negative\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from S&P Global Ratings: \par  \par OVERVIEW \par      -- Russia's military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continues to pose \par significant risks to the country's economic growth, financial stability, \par external position, and public finances. \par      -- We expect the government's debt service and borrowing needs will be \par fully covered by international financial support over at least the next 12 \par months; its ability to receive such support and make debt service payments, \par including to nonresidents, remains uninterrupted for now. \par      -- Our 'B-/B' long- and short-term foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ratings \par remain on CreditWatch with negative implications. \par      -- The CreditWatch status indicates that we could lower the ratings if \par the military conflict were to significantly weaken Ukraine's economy, its \par external liquidity, its financial system, or the government's administrative \par capacity. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par On March 11, 2022, S&P Global Ratings said its 'B-/B' long- and short-term \par foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings on Ukraine remain on \par CreditWatch with negative implications. \par  \par Our 'uaBBB-' national scale rating on Ukraine also remains on CreditWatch \par negative. \par  \par CREDITWATCH \par The CreditWatch negative reflects multiple risks to Ukraine's economy, \par external balances, public finances, and financial and political stability \par stemming from Russia's military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par  \par We expect to resolve the CreditWatch placement within 90 days. We could lower \par the ratings should we expect military actions to prevent the authorities from \par servicing commercial debt. The rating might also come under pressure should \par the conflict lead to a drain on Ukraine's external liquidity or a rise in \par fiscal contingent liabilities from its commercial banking system. \par  \par RATIONALE \par The ratings on Ukraine are constrained by Russia's military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  within \par the country, which bring about significant downside risks for the economy and \par financial sector. We see the potential for governance disruptions, putting \par commercial debt servicing at risk. The ratings are also constrained by \par Ukraine's low GDP per capita income and developing institutions. \par  \par Despite the conflict, Ukraine is continuing to honor its domestic and foreign \par debt service obligations and has pledged to continue doing so. On March 1, the \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  made a scheduled coupon payment of roughly $290 million on \par one of its eurobonds. At the same time, strong committed international \par financial support to Ukraine and still relatively high \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) \par reserves support the sovereign ratings. \par  \par Ukraine's GDP proved relatively resilient to the COVID-19 pandemic during both \par 2020 and 2021. However, the ongoing armed conflict with Russia has upended the \par gradually improving economic outlook. Ukraine now faces disruption to key \par economic sectors, significant loss of life, and broad damage to essential \par infrastructure. \par  \par The ultimate economic fallout caused by the current events is difficult to \par quantify, but a double-digit contraction in output for 2022 appears almost \par inevitable. Economic consequences from the conflict have already started to \par take hold via the suspension of commercial shipping from ports. This will \par weigh on Ukraine's export capacity and its ability to benefit from the \par currently favorable global prices for the country's key exports goods, \par including metals and wheat. \par  \par Output contraction, as well as military and emergency spending needs, will add \par significant pressures, increasing government financing requirements in 2022 \par severalfold compared with those expected before the conflict. \par  \par At the same time, even these elevated borrowing needs will likely be covered \par by different types of international financial support. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Bank of \par Ukraine\f28 \uc2 \f2   (\f28 \uc2 \f2 NBU\f28 \uc2 \f2  ) estimates the overall international financial and humanitarian \par support committed to date at about $15 billion (some 8.6% of 2021 GDP). This \par includes international loans, grants, and guarantees, namely: \par      -- A $1 billion guarantee from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  ; \par      -- A EUR1.2 billion emergency macrofinancial assistance program from the \par EU; \par      -- A loan for budgetary purposes of up to $500 million from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Canadian \par government\f28 \uc2 \f2  , in addition to a $120 million loan announced on Jan. 21, 2022; \par      -- A $500 million guarantee from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.K. government\f28 \uc2 \f2   to support Ukraine \par and mitigate the economic effects of the Russian military actions; \par      -- EUR300 million in financial aid from Germany; \par      -- $100 million in emergency loans from Japan; \par      -- $350 million disbursement from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 World Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2   to support short- and \par long-term financing needs. \par  \par The above come on top of Ukraine's $5 billion Stand-By Arrangement with the \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2  , of which, $2.2 billion remains undisbursed. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2   program was agreed in \par June 2020 and extended until end-June 2022. Ukraine has requested emergency \par financing of $1.4 billion under the \f28 \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2  's Rapid Financing Instrument. In \par addition, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Bank of Poland\f28 \uc2 \f2   has offered \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBU\f28 \uc2 \f2   a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swap \par equivalent to $950 million. The recent statements by the international \par community and international financial institutions suggest that the size of \par donor support could be further increased. We assume it will at least cover \par Ukraine's financing requirements in 2022. \par  \par Nevertheless, as the conflict continues, Ukraine's technical ability to use \par international financial support and meet its ongoing debt service obligations \par could erode. \par  \par The \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBU\f28 \uc2 \f2   has adopted several measures to mitigate exchange rate and financial \par market volatility as well as the resulting risks to macroeconomic stability \par stemming from the ongoing military conflict. These measures include the \par suspension of Ukraine's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market (except for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sales by customers), cash \par withdrawal limits, unlimited unsecured refinancing loans to banks, a \par moratorium on cross-border financial transactions (excluding those related to \par debt service), and a fixed \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 hryvnia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exchange rate. As of March 2, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBU\f28 \uc2 \f2   had \par official reserve assets of $27.5 billion. As per international convention, \par these are kept outside the country, mostly in G-7 counties. \par  \par Ahead of the military conflict, we estimated nonperforming loans in the \par Ukrainian banking system would decline to about 25% by year-end 2022 from 30% \par at end-2021. However, given the substantial fallout of current events on the \par private sector, the outlook for asset quality has deteriorated substantially, \par while confidence could also weigh on banks' liquidity positions. The potential \par recapitalization needs of Ukraine's state-owned banks will likely put \par additional pressure on the government's budget. \par  \par S&P Global Ratings acknowledges a high degree of uncertainty about the extent, \par outcome, and consequences of the military conflict between Russia and Ukraine. \par Irrespective of the duration of military hostilities, sanctions and related \par political risks are likely to remain in place for some time. Potential effects \par could include dislocated commodities markets--notably for oil and gas--supply \par chain disruptions, inflationary pressures, weaker growth, and capital market \par volatility. As the situation evolves, we will update our assumptions and \par estimates accordingly. See our macroeconomic and credit updates here: \par nflict". Note that the timing of publication for rating decisions on European \par issuers is subject to European regulatory requirements. \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA \par  \par      -- General Criteria: Environmental, Social, And Governance Principles In \par Credit Ratings, Oct. 10, 2021 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For National And Regional Scale Credit \par Ratings, June 25, 2018 \par      -- Criteria | Governments | Sovereigns: Sovereign Rating Methodology, \par Dec. 18, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Long-Term And Short-Term \par Ratings, April 7, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Principles Of Credit Ratings, Feb. 16, 2011 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology: Criteria For Determining Transfer And \par Convertibility Assessments, May 18, 2009 \par  \par RELATED RESEARCH \par      -- Russia-Ukraine Military Conflict: Key Takeaways From Our Articles, \par March 8, 2022 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings Score Snapshot, March 8, 2022 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings List, March 3, 2022 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings History, March 3, 2022 \par      -- Cyber Threat Brief: How Worried Should We Be About Cyber Attacks On \par Ukraine? Feb. 22, 2022 \par      -- Sovereign Risk Indicators, Dec. 13, 2022; a free interactive version \par is available at  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.spratings.com/sri"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.spratings.com/sri }}}\uc2 \f2  \par      -- Default, Transition, and Recovery: 2020 Annual Sovereign Default And \par Rating Transition Study, April 12, 2021 \par  \par In accordance with our relevant policies and procedures, the Rating Committee \par was composed of analysts that are qualified to vote in the committee, with \par sufficient experience to convey the appropriate level of knowledge and \par understanding of the methodology applicable (see 'Related Criteria And \par Research'). At the onset of the committee, the chair confirmed that the \par information provided to the Rating Committee by the primary analyst had been \par distributed in a timely manner and was sufficient for Committee members to \par make an informed decision. \par  \par After the primary analyst gave opening remarks and explained the \par recommendation, the Committee discussed key rating factors and critical issues \par in accordance with the relevant criteria. Qualitative and quantitative risk \par factors were considered and discussed, looking at track-record and forecasts. \par  \par The committee's assessment of the key rating factors is reflected in the \par Ratings Score Snapshot above. \par  