\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 If Russian attack is not imminent, why does Ukraine ask for weapons?\b0\par\par\uc2 1,289 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 US said it will no longer refer to a possible Russian invasion of Ukraine as "imminent" after complaints from the Kiev government. But Ukraine continues to ask for help from allies to receive weapons. Why? For weeks, the world has been watching the tensions between Ukraine and Russia unfold. The United States even classified an attack by Moscow on Kiev as "imminent. The Ukrainian government has tried to cool things down and calm the population, but at the same time welcomes Western leaders and calls for arms shipments. If a Russian attack is not likely in the short term, why is Ukraine asking for help and receiving weapons?\par \par \uc2 Moscow has stationed about 100,000 troops near the borders with Ukraine, increasing speculation that it might invade the neighboring country. In 2014, Russia annexed the Ukrainian peninsula of Crimea to its territory.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  warns that this number of soldiers could quickly rise to 175,000. Western intelligence analysts say that troops are increasingly in possession of the logistics needed to launch an attack, including blood supplies to Russian field hospitals near the Ukrainian border. At the same time, Russia is holding joint exercises in Belarus, involving 30,000 troops, \uc2 NATO\uc2  estimates.\par \par \uc2 In response, Ukraine has requested and begun receiving weapons from Western countries in recent weeks: anti-tank systems from the US and UK, and ammunition and anti-aircraft missiles from the Baltic States. The \uc2 European Union\uc2  (\uc2 EU\uc2 ) has pledged more than 1 billion euros in assistance.\par \par \uc2 The central question is whether this is just a rash move by Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  in an attempt to draw the West's attention to his demand that Ukraine never be allowed to join \uc2 NATO\uc2  or whether Russia's leader really intends to launch an attack on Ukraine. And if there is indeed an attack, whether it would take the form of a localized \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or a full-scale invasion.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Criticism of the USA\par \par \uc2 On February 2, White House press secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  announced that the term "imminent" would no longer be used to describe the threat of a Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Not because the objective assessment of U.S. intelligence has changed, but because the term may inadvertently suggest U.S. certainty about Putin's intentions. The use of the term and the tough rhetoric of Joe Biden's administration have also caused significant friction with the Ukrainian government.\par \par \uc2 "I am the president of Ukraine and I am here on the ground, I think I understand the details better than any other president," was Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelensky\uc2 's message to foreign journalists who asked him to explain the gap between U.S. messaging and his government's more cautious line.\par \par \uc2 While Washington warned about the acute threat of Russian invasion, Zelensky told Ukrainians that the risks were no greater than before. All that changed, Zelensky claimed, was the sudden increase in international media attention.\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainian president's statement was all the more surprising because the country's leaders, including Zelensky himself, have spent years urging the West not to underestimate the threat posed by Russia to Ukraine's independence. Not only that: Kiev has assembled an impressive wish list of Western weapons systems it says it needs to prevent Russia from attacking.\par \par \uc2 The differences between Kiev and Washington are more about "stylistics and emphasis" than anything else, argued political scientist Volodymyr Fesenko. He pointed out that since Russia's annexation of Crimea in 2014, Moscow has increased its permanent military presence along Ukraine's borders and held large-scale exercises every European spring and summer. As a result, Ukrainians, Fesenko explains, have learned to live with the constant risk of escalation.\par \par \uc2 All the current talk of an attack has already had a very real impact in Ukraine. "The war hasn't broken out yet, but war is already being waged in m\u237\'00\'EDsia, and this is having a negative impact on the Ukrainian economy," Fesenko told DW.\par \par \uc2 How have previous escalations been handled?\par \par \uc2 This is not the first time Russia has used troop movements to escalate tensions with Ukraine. In March and April 2021, Russia massed tens of thousands of troops on the Ukrainian borders and held Navy exercises in the Black Sea.\par \par \uc2 The move caused international concern, but fears of imminent conflict were less acute than at present. At that time, Russian forces did not have the logistics to launch a large-scale attack and Russia had not formulated any concrete demands.\par \par \uc2 In the spring of 2021, Biden's offer of a one-on-one summit with Putin, whom he had called a "killer" only a few weeks earlier, was enough for Russia to shut down the maneuvers.\par \par \uc2 Now, the mere offer of dialogue is unlikely to be enough to resolve the tensions. The Biden-Putin summit in Geneva in June 2021 did little to advance Russia's goals.\par \par \uc2 This time, Moscow issued a list of demands, including a ban on Ukraine joining \uc2 NATO\uc2  and a commitment by the Atlantic Alliance to withdraw troops and military equipment from member states in Eastern Europe. \uc2 NATO\uc2  has already ruled out meeting any of these demands.\par \par \uc2 That leaves talks on intermediate-range missile control in Europe as the only part of Russia's demands with any chance of progress.\par \par \uc2 Observers expect that any such agreement on arms control, even if reached, is unlikely to be a sufficient diplomatic victory for the Kremlin to be willing to de-escalate.\par \par \uc2 How much is the escalation costing Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 Even without a single shot fired, talk of imminent war in Ukraine is already damaging international investor confidence in the country. That's part of the reason why President Zelensky is so keen to tone down the rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 So far, Ukraine's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has held up well, losing less than 10 percent of its value against the euro in recent weeks - although this has not happened without the help of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has spent more than a billion euros to halt the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fall.\par \par \uc2 The most relevant indirect effect on the economy so far, says analyst Sergey Fursa, has been Ukraine's ability to borrow money on international markets. In the current situation, bond auctions would be doomed to failure, Fursa explains.\par \par \uc2 Evidence is mounting that international companies would be dropping plans to invest in Ukraine, as some companies in the country are following the example of some Western embassies and evacuating employees from the Ukrainian capital.\par \par \uc2 Quantifying the value of such lost investments is difficult. However, as the current escalation unfolds, analysts warn that the continuing uncertainty over Ukraine's security could become a permanent brake on the country's economic development.\par \par \uc2 Is there an upside for Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 In just one week, the leaders of Turkey, the UK, and the Netherlands visited Kiev, with promises of military supplies. It was an unprecedented show of support and something that Ukraine in normal times would celebrate as a diplomatic victory.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine is truly on Europe's and \uc2 NATO\uc2 's agenda - so fear that Russia's demands will be taken seriously.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine has received weapons and promises of future deliveries not only from the United States, but also from other \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries, such as the United Kingdom, which previously had not supplied lethal weapons.\par \par \uc2 But this does not change the fact that the value of the equipment offered to Ukraine is still modest compared to U.S. support for Israel or Afghanistan before the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  takeover last year.\par \par \uc2 As for \uc2 NATO\uc2  membership, the alliance has refused to rule out Ukraine's entry, but has not given any hope for a short-term membership either.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220205ei25008xw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
