1 January and 31 December     1,308        -      6,355   7,663 \par -----------------------------  -------  -------  ---------  ------ \par Carrying amount \par At 1 January                         -      768      7,164   7,932 \par -----------------------------  -------  -------  ---------  ------ \par At 31 December                       -      814      6,998   7,812 \par -----------------------------  -------  -------  ---------  ------ \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 4. (c) Impairment test for property, plant and equipment\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A review was undertaken at the reporting date of the carrying amounts of property, plant and equipment to determine whether there was any indication of a trigger that may have led to these assets suffering an impairment loss. Following this review impairment triggers were noted in relation to the Russian assets (see Note 4 (e)) and the Hungarian assets (see Note 4 (f)). In respect of the Group's Ukrainian assets, impairment triggers were noted in 2015 and a full impairment review was completed, however no impairment triggers were noted in 2016 (see Note 4 (d)).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As there is no readily available market for the Group's oil and gas properties, fair value is derived as the net present value of the estimated future cash flows arising from the continued use of the assets, incorporating assumptions that a typical market participant would take into account.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The value in use of an oil and gas property is generally lower than its Fair Value Less Costs of Disposal ('FVLCD') as value in use reflects only those cash flows expected to be derived from the asset in its current condition. FVLCD includes appraisal and development expenditure that a market participant would consider likely to enhance the productive capacity of an asset and optimise future cash flows. Consequently, the Group determines recoverable amount based on FVLCD using a Discounted Cash Flow ('DCF') methodology.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The DCF was derived by estimating discounted after tax cash flows for each CGU based on estimates that a typical market participant would use in valuing such assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The impairment tests compared the recoverable amount of the respective CGUs noted below to the respective carrying values of their associated assets. The estimates of FVLCD meet the definition of level three fair value measurements as they are determined from unobservable inputs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 4. (d) Impairment test for the Ukrainian oil and gas assets - 2015 information\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Change in spelling of Ukrainian production licences\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the 2016 financial statements, all of the names of the Company's Ukrainian production licences have been changed to their Ukrainian language spelling. A list of the changes made from the previous years' financial statement is as follows: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    2016 financial statements               Previous years' financial statements \par    Rudenkivske                                        Rudenkovskoye \par    Ignativske                                              Ignatovskoye \par    Movchanivske                                       Molchanovskoye \par    Novomykolaivske                                Novo-Nikolaevskoye \par    Zaplavska                                             Zaplavskoye \par    Elyzavetivske                                        Elizavetovskoye \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2015\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 During 2015, the geopolitical situation in Ukraine, the economic impact of the devaluation of the Ukrainian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hryvnia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the uncertainty about the political, fiscal and economic outlook increased the Company's post tax discount rate used in its DCF calculations for impairment testing on the Ukrainian assets. The post tax discount rate increased from 17.2% to 20.0%. Together with the continued decline in international oil and gas prices during 2015, these constituted an impairment trigger and accordingly an impairment test was undertaken.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Poltava Petroleum Company ('PPC'), a wholly owned subsidiary of JKX, holds 100% interest in five production licences (Ignativske, Movchanivske, Rudenkivske, Novomykolaivske, Elyzavetivske) and one exploration licence (Zaplavska) in the Poltava region of Ukraine.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Ignativske, Movchanivske, Rudenkivske, Novomykolaivske production licences contain one or more distinct fields which, together with the Zaplavska exploration licence, form the Novomykolaivske Complex ('NNC').\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Elyzavetivske production licence is located 45km from the Novomykolaivske Complex and has its own gas production facilities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ukrainian Cash Generating Units ('CGUs')\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In respect of the Group's Ukraine assets the NNC forms a single CGU as these contain oil and gas fields which are serviced by a single processing facility and do not have separately identifiable cash inflows. In addition they have commonality of facilities, personnel and services.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Elyzavetivske licence also has its own separate processing facilities and separately identifiable cash flows and therefore is a distinct CGU for the purpose of the impairment test. During 2015 an extension to the Elyzavetivske production licence was awarded to PPC which included the West Mashivska field. Due to the proximity of the West Mashivska field to the Elyzavetivske plant, production will be tied back to the Elyzavetivske processing facilities and therefore forms part of this CGU.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In accordance with IAS 36, the impairment review was undertaken in US$ being the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in which future cash flows from NNC and Elyzavetivske will be generated.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Key Assumptions 2015 - NNC and Elyzavetivske\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The key assumptions used in the impairment testing were:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Production profiles: these were based on the latest available information provided by independent reserve engineers, DeGolyer & MacNaughton, as at 31 December 2014 adjusted for 2015 production volumes and data and reassessed internally. Such information included 3P reserves for NNC and Elyzavetivske (including the West Mashivska extension) of 28.4 MMboe and 5.0 MMboe, respectively.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Economic life of field: it was assumed that the title to the licences is retained and that the NNC licence term will be successfully extended beyond its current 2024 expiration date through to the economic life of the field (expected to be around 2032). The economic life of the Elyzavetivske field is currently expected to be around 2023.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Gas prices: during 2015 Ukraine acquired the ability to purchase gas from Europe rather than being completely dependent on Russia for imports. As such, Ukrainian gas prices are expected to be more aligned with European gas prices in future but also influenced by Russian-Ukrainian border price and international oil prices. The gas price used for 2016 is based on current and forecast gas prices realised by PPC. For the following six years a forward gas price curve was used with gas prices increasing at 2.8% thereafter.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Oil prices: the Company used a forward price curve for the next six years and an increase of 2.8% per annum thereafter.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Production taxes: the Company has assumed production tax rates of 29% for gas and 45% for oil which were introduced by the government on 1 January 2016.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Capital and operating costs: these were based on current operating and capital costs in Ukraine for both projects. Estimates were provided by third parties and supported by estimates from our own specialists, where necessary.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Post tax nominal discount rate of 20%. This was based on a Capital Asset Pricing Model analysis consistent with that used in previous impairment reviews.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Based on the key assumptions set out above:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- the NNC's oil and gas assets were impaired by $49.6m after significant erosion of the headroom from the prior year due to the increase in discount rate applied, the international oil and gas price decline and the new expectation that prices will remain lower for longer.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Elyzavetivske's recoverable amount (including the West Mashivska extension) exceeds its carrying value by $34.9m and therefore Elyzavetivske's oil and gas assets were not impaired.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par JKX Oil & Gas PLC Final Results to 31 December -15-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Any impairment is dependent on judgement used in determining the most appropriate basis for the assumptions and estimates made by management, particularly in relation to the key assumptions described above. Sensitivity analysis to likely and potential changes in key assumptions has therefore been provided below.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The impact on the impairment calculation of applying different assumptions to gas prices, production volumes, production tax rates, future capital expenditure and post-tax discount rates, all other inputs remaining equal, would be as follows:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sensitivity analysis 2015 for the NNC and Elyzavetivske \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                                                       Elyzavetivske \par                                                       NNC           Increase/(decrease) \par                                               Increase/(decrease)      in impairment \par                                                  in impairment           headroom \par                                                    of $49.6m             of $34.9m \par                                                   for NNC CGU        for Elyzavetivske \par                                                       $m                  CGU $m \par ----------------------  -------------------  --------------------  -------------------- \par Impact if gas \par  price:                 increased by 20%            (37.6)                 13.1 \par  reduced by 20%                                      37.6                 (13.1) \par  ------------------------------------------  --------------------  -------------------- \par Impact if gas \par  production volumes:    increased by 10%            (24.0)                 6.7 \par  decreased by 10%                                    24.0                 (6.7) \par  ------------------------------------------  --------------------  -------------------- \par Impact if future \par  capital expenditure:   increased by 20%             27.5                 (3.9) \par  decreased by 20%                                   (27.5)                 3.9 \par  ------------------------------------------  --------------------  -------------------- \par                         increased by 2 \par Impact if post-tax       percentage points \par  discount rate:          to 22.0%                    9.5                  (1.8) \par  decreased by 2 \par   percentage points \par   to 18.0%                                          (11.0)                 2.0 \par  ------------------------------------------  --------------------  -------------------- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 4. (e) Impairment test for Yuzhgazenergie LLC ('YGE'), Russia\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Following the 2007 acquisition of YGE in Russia, a technical and environmental re-evaluation of YGE's Koshekhablskoye gas field redevelopment was undertaken by the Group. The re-evaluation resulted in a revised development plan and production profile. The development plan and production profile have continued to be refined since that time.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For purposes of testing for impairment triggers of YGE's non-current assets, the Company took account of developments since the last test for impairment in 2014, based on the assessment of FVLCD.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Russia, the regulated maximum industrial gas price in Russia was increased by 1.95% from 1 July 2016 however, following a renegotiation of the gas sales contract, the Company agreed a reduction of 9.5% to the price at which it sells its gas to its sole gas customer in Russia in return for a longer-term "take or pay" agreement. This price reduction had not been anticipated in previous impairment reviews.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company is seeking to engage other buyers of its gas in southern Russia to improve gas realisations there and broaden its customer base.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This revision to our estimate of the future Russian gas prices constituted an impairment trigger. Accordingly an impairment test was undertaken.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In accordance with IAS 36, the impairment review was been undertaken in Russian Roubles, which is the functional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of YGE.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Key Assumptions - YGE\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The key assumptions used in the impairment testing were:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Production profiles: these were based on the latest available information provided by independent reserve engineers, DeGolyer & MacNaughton, at 31 December 2016. Such information included 2P reserves for YGE of 79.5 MMboe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Economic life of field: it was assumed that YGE will be successful in extending the licence term beyond its current 2026 expiration to the economic life of the field (expected to be around 2048). The discounted cash flow methodology used has not taken account of any opportunities that may exist to extract reserves in a shorter timeframe by investing to increase the current plant capacity.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Gas prices: for 2017 these were based on the gas sales agreement that the Company had negotiated with its sole gas customer for the forecast gas production in 2017. The gas price is expected to remain at the same level through to 1 July 2017.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Gas prices: from 1 July 2017 and annually thereafter, the gas prices have been increased by Rouble inflation of between 3.1% and 4.0% through to 2023, and estimated Russian inflation of 5.1% thereafter.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Capital and operating costs: these were based on current operating and capital costs in Russia, project estimates provided by third parties and supported by estimates from our own specialists, where necessary.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Post tax nominal Rouble discount rate of 13.2%. This was based on a Capital Asset Pricing Model analysis consistent with that used in previous impairment reviews.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Based on the key assumptions set out above YGE's recoverable amount exceeds it carrying amount by $14.7m and therefore YGE's Koshekhablskoye gas field was not impaired.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Any impairment is dependent on judgement used in determining the most appropriate basis for the assumptions and estimates made by management, particularly in relation to the key assumptions described above. Sensitivity analysis to likely and potential changes in key assumptions has therefore been reviewed below.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The impact on the impairment calculation of applying different assumptions to gas prices, production, future capital expenditure and post-tax discount rates, all other inputs remaining equal, would be as follows:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sensitivity Analysis \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                                     Increase/(decrease) \par                                                           in impairment \par                                                             headroom of \par                                                              $14.7m for \par                                                          Yuzhgazenergie \par                                                                     CGU \par                                                                      $m \par ----------------------  --------------------------  ------------------- \par Impact if Adygean       growth rates increased \par  gas price:              by 10% annually                    13 \par  growth rates reduced \par   by 10% annually                                          (13) \par  -------------------------------------------------  ------------------- \par Impact if production \par  volumes:               Increased by 10%                    24 \par  Decreased by 10%                                          (25) \par  -------------------------------------------------  ------------------- \par Impact if future \par  capital expenditure:   Increased by 20%                   (12) \par  Decreased by 20%                                           12 \par  -------------------------------------------------  ------------------- \par Impact if post-tax      Increased by 1 percentage \par  discount rate:          point to 14.2%                     (9) \par  Decreased by 1 percentage \par   point to 12.2%                                            10 \par  -------------------------------------------------  ------------------- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 4. (f) Impairment test for Hungarian oil and gas assets\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungarian property plant and equipment - Foly\u243\'00\'F3part Energia Kft ('FEN')\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company now holds a 100% interest in six development licences (Mining Plots) through its wholly owned Hungarian subsidiary, Foly\u243\'00\'F3part Energia Kft. The Hajdunanas IV Mining Plot ('HMP') (previously Hernad I licence) contains two suspended wells which experienced an unexpected decline in production rates in 2013.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In December 2016, well Hn-2ST (sidetrack) was 