,590 \par  Non-Current \par  Convertible bonds due 2018               22,719    29,449        30,837 \par --------------------------------------  --------  --------  ------------ \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Convertible bonds due 2018\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On 19 February 2013 the Company successfully completed the placing of $40m of guaranteed unsubordinated convertible bonds with institutional investors which are due 2018 raising cash of $37.2m net of issue costs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bonds have an annual coupon of 8 per cent per annum payable semi-annually in arrears. The Bonds are convertible into ordinary shares of the Company at any time from 1 April 2013 up until seven days prior to their maturity on 19 February 2018 at a conversion price of 76.29 pence per Ordinary Share, unless the Company settles the conversion notice by paying the Bondholder the Cash Alternative Amount (see below).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Interest, after the deduction of issue costs and the inclusion of the redemption premium, will be charged to the income statement using an effective rate of 18.0%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cash Alternative Amount\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At the option of the Company, the conversion notice in respect of the Bonds can be settled in cash rather than shares, the Cash Alternative Amount payable is based on the Volume Weighted Average Price of the Company's shares prior to the conversion notice.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Credit Facility\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On 31 March 2011, Poltava Petroleum Company ('PPC'), our subsidiary in Ukraine, entered into a reducing credit facility agreement with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Cr\u233\'00\'E9dit Agricole\f28 \uc2 \f2  CIB (France) secured by indemnity provided by the parent company, \f28 \uc2 \f2 JKX Oil & Gas plc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The credit facility was for a maximum of the Ukrainian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hryvnia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  equivalent of $15.0m and expired on 30 June 2015; discussions are currently underway to renew this facility. All provisions contained in the credit facility documentation were negotiated on normal commercial and customary terms for such finance arrangements. The interest was calculated at prevailing \f28 \uc2 \f2 Cr\u233\'00\'E9dit Agricole\f28 \uc2 \f2  CIB (France) bank rate plus a margin.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    10.   Derivatives \par  \par                                                   30 June   30 June   31 December \par                                                      2015      2014          2014 \par  Non-current derivative financial instruments        $000      $000          $000 \par -----------------------------------------------  --------  --------  ------------ \par  At beginning of the period/year                    1,037    10,109        10,109 \par  Partial settlement of derivative liability         (787)         -             - \par  Fair value loss/(gain) during the period/year      3,969   (6,078)       (9,072) \par -----------------------------------------------  --------  --------  ------------ \par  At the end of the period/year                      4,219     4,031         1,037 \par -----------------------------------------------  --------  --------  ------------ \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Convertible bonds due 2018 - embedded derivatives\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Coupon Makewhole\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Upon conversion of a Bond prior to 19 February 2016 the Company is required to pay an amount of interest equal to the aggregate interest which would have been payable on the principal amount of the Bond if such Bond had been outstanding until 19 February 2016.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bondholder Put Option\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bondholders have the right to require the Company to redeem the following number of Bonds on the following future dates together with accrued and unpaid interest to (but excluding) such dates:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  Redemption Date    Maximum number of Bonds to be redeemed \par -----------------  ------------------------------------------------ \par                     25% of the Bonds, having an aggregate principal \par  19 February 2016    amount of $10,000,000 \par -----------------  ------------------------------------------------ \par  19 February 2017   all outstanding Bonds \par -----------------  ------------------------------------------------ \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Current liabilities include $10.1m (31 December 2014: $4.1m) in respect of the put option available to bondholders on 19 February 2016 (31 December 2014: 19 February 2015). Bonds with a principal amount of $4m were redeemed on 19 February 2015 in addition to an early redemption premium of $0.2m in accordance with the terms and conditions of the bond. None of the bondholders exercised their option to put 10% of the outstanding principal of the bonds on 19 February 2014.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Company Call Option\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company can redeem the Bonds early in full but not in part at their principal amount together with accrued interest at any time on or after 19 February 2017 if the Volume Weighted Average Price of the Company's shares over a specified period equal or exceed 130 per cent of the principal amount of the Bonds; or if the aggregate principal amount of the bonds outstanding is less than 15% of the aggregate principal amount originally issued.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fixed exchange rate\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Sterling-US Dollar exchange rate is fixed at GBP1/$1.5809 for the conversion and other features.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    11.   Financial instruments \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fair values of financial assets and financial liabilities - Group [ 29-07-15 0600GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Set out below is a comparison by category of carrying amounts and fair values of the Group's financial instruments. Fair value is the amount at which a financial instrument could be exchanged in an arms length transaction. Where available, market values have been used (this excludes short term assets and liabilities).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                                  Book and      Book \par                                                   Fair Value    Value          Fair Value \par  \par                                                  30 June       31 December     31 December \par                                                   2015          2014           2014 \par                                                  $000          $000          $'000 \par  Financial assets \par  Cash and cash equivalent and restricted cash \par   (note 7)                                       22,410        25,943        25,943 \par  Trade receivables - classified as loans and \par   receivables                                    2,389         3,116         3,116 \par  Other receivables - classified as loans and \par   receivables                                    1,304         1,043         1,043 \par  Held-to-maturity financial investments (note \par   8) - classified as loans and receivables       -             2,700         3,262 \par ----------------------------------------------  ------------  ------------  -------------- \par  Financial liabilities \par  Trade payables - carried at amortised cost      2,181         1,694         1,694 \par  Other payables - carried at amortised cost      686           1,416         1,416 \par  Borrowings - credit facility (note 9)           -             1,522         1,522 \par  Borrowings - convertible bond due 2018 (note \par   9) - at amortised cost                         10,068        4,068         4,068 \par  Borrowings - convertible bond due 2018 (note \par   9) - at amortised cost                         22,719        30,837        30,837 \par  Derivatives - fair value through profit or \par   loss (note 10)                                 4,219         1,037         1,037 \par ----------------------------------------------  ------------  ------------  -------------- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Financial liabilities measured at amortised cost are carried at $35.7m (31 December 2014: $39.5m). The Group's borrowings at 30 June 2015 relate entirely to the convertible bond due 2018.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fair value hierarchy\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Derivatives\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At the period end the Group's derivative financial instrument related to various embedded derivatives within the convertible bonds due in 2018 (note 10). The value of the derivative was calculated at inception and the reporting date using the Monte Carlo simulation methodology and Black-Scholes formula, respectively, and the Company historic share price and volatility, treasury rates and other estimations. As it was derived from inputs that are not from observable market data it was grouped into level 3 within the fair value measurement hierarchy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The main assumptions used in valuation of the derivative conversion option as at 30 June 2015 were:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    --      underlying share price of: GBP0.275 (31 December 2014: GBP0.120); \par    --      GBP/US$ spot rate of 1.5712 (31 December 2014: GBP1/$1.5577); \par    --      historic volatility of 98.35% (31 December 2014: 70.70%); \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- risk free rate based on 2.64 year (31 December 2014: 3.14 year) \f28 \uc2 \f2 US Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  rate of 0.638% (31 December 2014: 0.897%).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A 10% increase/decrease in Company's historic share price volatility would have resulted in an increase in the fair value loss for the period of $0.9m (31 December 2014: reduction in the fair value gain for the year of $0.3m) and a decrease in the fair value loss of $0.8m (31 December 2014: increase in the fair value gain of $0.2m) respectively, assuming that all other variables remain constant.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Credit risk - Group\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group has policies in place to ensure that sales of products are made to customers with appropriate credit worthiness. The Group limits credit risk by assessing creditworthiness of potential counterparties before entering into transactions with them and continuing to evaluate their creditworthiness after transactions have been initiated. Where appropriate, the use of prepayment for product sales limits the exposure to credit risk. There is no difference between the carrying amount of trade and other receivables and the maximum credit risk exposure.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The maximum financial exposure due to credit risk on the Group's financial assets, representing the sum of cash and cash equivalents, trade receivables, held to maturity financial investments and other current assets, as at 30 June 2015 was $26.1m (31 December 2014: $32.8m).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Capital management - Group\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Directors determine the appropriate capital structure of the Group specifically, how much is raised from shareholders (equity) and how much is borrowed from financial institutions (debt) in order to finance the Group's business strategy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group's policy as to the level of equity capital and reserves is to ensure that it maintains a strong financial position and low gearing ratio which provides financial flexibility to continue as a going concern and to maximise shareholder value. The capital structure of the Group consists of shareholders' equity together with net debt. The Group's funding requirements are met through a combination of debt, equity and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cash flow.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Net debt\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Net debt comprises: borrowings disclosed in note 9 and total cash in note 7, and excludes derivatives. Equity attributable to the shareholders of the Company comprises issued capital, capital reserves and retained earnings, (see Condensed consolidated statement of changes in equity).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The capital structure of the Group is as follows:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                                                   30 June   31 December \par                                                                      2015          2014 \par                                                                      $000          $000 \par ----------------------------------------------------  -------------------  ------------ \par  Borrowings (note 9)                                             (10,068)       (5,590) \par  Convertible bonds due 2018 - Non-current liability \par   (note 9)                                                       (22,719)      (30,837) \par  Total cash (note 7)                                               22,410        25,943 \par  Government treasury bills (note 8)                                     -         2,700 \par ----------------------------------------------------  -------------------  ------------ \par  Net debt                                                        (10,377)       (7,784) \par ----------------------------------------------------  -------------------  ------------ \par  Total equity                                                     270,854       281,348 \par ----------------------------------------------------  -------------------  ------------ \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Following the issue of $40m of convertible bonds in February 2013, the primary capital risk to the Group is the level of indebtedness. The convertible bond includes a financial covenant which limits the Group's indebtedness (excluding the bonds themselves and the $15.0m \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Agricole\f28 \uc2 \f2  facility) in respect of any new borrowings (in addition to the bond amount) to three times 12-month free cash flow based on the most recently published consolidated financial statements. During the period/year the Group complied with this financial covenant.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Liquidity risk - Group\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The treasury function is responsible for liquidity, funding and settlement management under policies approved by the Board of Directors. Liquidity needs are monitored using regular forecasting of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cash flows and financing commitments. The Group maintains a mixture of cash and cash equivalents and committed facilities in order to ensure sufficient funding for business requirements.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Significant restrictions\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cash and short-term deposits held in Ukraine are subject to local exchange control regulations. These regulations provide for restrictions on exporting capital from Ukraine.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cash and short term deposits included within this consolidated financial information to which these restrictions apply is $6.7m (31 December 2014: $0.5m).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Temporary capital controls established by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Bank of Ukraine\f28 \uc2 \f2  ('NBU') on 1 December 2014 remain in place in an attempt by the Ukrainian government to safeguard the economy and protect \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves in the short term.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On 4 March 2015 a number of new NBU Resolutions were implemented with immediate effect (NBU No. 160 dated 3 March 2015; Resolution of the NBU No. 161 dated 3 March 2015; Resolution of the NBU No. 154 dated 2 March 2015).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Resolutions extended the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  control restrictions implemented in Ukraine on 1 December 2014 and introduced additional measures which have the impact of restricting the remittance of funds to foreign investors under certain conditions and bans the transfer of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hryvnia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to purchase Ukrainian Government bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The restrictions were initially ef