\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 4-Ukraine under pressure from  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  fall, gas supply threat\b0\par\par\uc2 1,012 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2015\par 18:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 * Pro-Russian rebels pull back heavy guns\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  says it too early to follow suit\par \par \uc2 * Ukrainian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading halted\par \par \uc2 * Putin makes new threat on gas supplies\par \par \uc2 By Natalia Zinets and Anton Zverev\par \par \uc2 KIEV/MAKIYVKA, Ukraine, Feb 25 (Reuters) - Ukraine came under increasing economic pressure from a collapsing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a threat to its gas supplies from the Kremlin on Wednesday, just as a long-awaited ceasefire took hold in the east.\par \par \uc2 As the truce appeared to be coming into force, the Ukrainian army reported no combat fatalities in the past 24 hours, but the news did nothing to halt a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slump that forced the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ban most trading before \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In rebel-held eastern \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 , pro-Russian separatists were withdrawing heavy guns from the front. Kiev said it was too early to do likewise, but its acknowledgement that most of the front was quiet suggested it too could implement a truce that had appeared stillborn when the rebels launched a major offensive last week.\par \par \uc2 The cautious good news from the front has come amid dire economic consequences for a country teetering on bankruptcy.\par \par \uc2 With the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in free fall as investors flee, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  called a halt by banning nearly all commercial \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading until the end of the week.\par \par \uc2 It later jumped into the market to buy dollars, reversing a free-fall in the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  that it called "irrational".\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Arseny Yatseniuk said the ban was bad for the economy. He had learned about it on the Internet and would demand an explanation from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief \uc2 Valeria Gontareva\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Gontareva said there was no fundamental reason for the panic in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 At the day's end the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced it had bought $80 million at an official rate of 28.046 to the dollar, close to the rate at the start of the week and 12.8 percent higher than the close after a plunge on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Exchange kiosks in Kiev were selling limited amounts of dollars for 39 \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnias\b0 \highlight\uc2 , around 20 percent worse than rates advertised in the windows of commercial banks where dollars were not available.\par \par \uc2 A construction worker exchanging dollars at a kiosk in a grocery shop in return for a bag filled with thousands of \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , laughed and told shoppers: "Soon we will have to walk around with suitcases for cash, like in the 1990s."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost at least half its value so far this year after halving over the course of 2014.\par \par \uc2 In a potential new blow, President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  warned that Russia would halt gas supplies to Ukraine, for the fourth time in a decade, if Moscow did not receive advance payment.\par \par \uc2 That could disrupt flows to Europe, which receives around a third of its gas from Russia, with 40 percent shipped via Ukraine. However, Russia cut off gas to Ukraine for six months last year without affecting Europe.\par \par \uc2 Criticising Ukraine for cutting off gas to eastern regions under the control of the pro-Russian separatists, Putin said: "Imagine these people will be left without gas in winter. Not only that there is famine ... It smells of genocide."\par \par \uc2 "We hope ... that gas supplies will not be interrupted. But this does not depend only on us, it depends on the financial discipline of our Ukrainian partners," Putin said.\par \par \uc2 REBEL ADVANCE\par \par \uc2 News that no Ukrainian troops had died at the front was by far the most unambiguous signal yet that the French- and German-brokered truce is now holding.\par \par \uc2 The rebels had initially spurned the ceasefire, insisting it did not apply to their main target, the town of Debaltseve, which they stormed last week.\par \par \uc2 Kiev has since accused the separatists of reinforcing for a possible further assault deeper into territory the Kremlin calls "New Russia". But for now, the fighters appear determined to be seen to implement the agreement.\par \par \uc2 Reuters journalists, operating independently in rebel-held territory, saw columns of howitzers driven away from the front in several locations on Wednesday after initial moves on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 A column of 24 self-propelled howitzers headed away from the front through the city of Makiyvka adjacent to the main rebel stronghold, Donetsk. Another five were spotted driving away from the front near Yenakiyve, further north.\par \par \uc2 The rebels have promised that the Organisation for Security and Cooperation in Europe will soon be able to verify that they have removed all heavy guns.\par \par \uc2 The OSCE says it cannot yet verify the withdrawal because the sides have not provided data on how many guns were in place before the truce. The European security body reported some shelling and shooting at various locations, including near Shyrokyne, a coastal town where Kiev has also reported fighting.\par \par \uc2 The Kiev military nevertheless said the number of ceasefire violations had "significantly decreased" for a second straight night. No shooting was recorded at all in the Donetsk, Luhansk and Mariupol areas, it said. Overall, rebels had fired shells and mortars 15 times and opened fire four times with light weapons during the 24-hour period.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian military spokesman Andriy Lysenko said: "For now there is still no order on the withdrawal of weapons, as the fighters have not yet fulfilled the first point of the Minsk agreement, to cease fire."\par \par \uc2 Kiev fears the rebels, backed by Russian troops, may now be planning to capture Mariupol, a port of 500,000 people. Moscow denies aiding its sympathisers in east Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Secretary of State \uc2 John Kerry\uc2  said additional sanctions against Russia were "teed up" should events in eastern Ukraine require a significant response.\par \par \uc2 Britain ruled out deploying combat troops to Ukraine, a day after it said it was sending 75 military trainers to help the Ukrainian army. Poland said it intended to send military instructors to train Ukrainian soldiers. (Additional reporting by Pavel Polityuk and Peter Graff in Kiev; Writing by Peter Graff; Editing by Mark Trevelyan and Giles Elgood) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-2-25T18:57:14.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150225eb2p00dio\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
