\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 10-Separatists ignore ultimatum, tighten grip on east Ukraine\b0\par\par\uc2 1,252 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 April 2014\par 03:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Obama speaks to Putin               \par \par \uc2 * Rebels ignore deadline to surrender arms               \par \par \uc2 * EU step up sanctions against Russia               \par \par \uc2 * Russian stocks and rouble fall sharply   (Updates with White House quote, adds links)               \par \par \uc2 By Thomas Grove and Gabriela Baczynska               \par \par \uc2 SLAVIANSK/DONETSK, Ukraine, April 14 (Reuters) - Pro-Russian separatists on Monday ignored an ultimatum to leave occupied government buildings in eastern Ukraine and instead seized more buildings as the government failed to follow through on a threatened military crackdown.               \par \par \uc2 U.S. President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  criticized Russia in a telephone conversation with Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  later on Monday, saying Moscow's actions in Ukraine were not conducive to a diplomatic solution.               \par \par \uc2 The Kremlin said it had requested the call. The White House said the call was frank and direct.               \par \par \uc2 "President Putin called on \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  to do his utmost to use the opportunities that the United States has to prevent the use of force, and bloodshed," the Kremlin said in a statement.               \par \par \uc2 The White House said Obama urged Russia to use its influence to get separatists in the country to stand down.               \par \par \uc2 "The president emphasized that all irregular forces in the country need to lay down their arms, and he urged President Putin to use his influence with these armed, pro-Russian groups to convince them to depart the buildings they have seized," the White House said in a statement.               \par \par \uc2 Pro-Russian rebels have seized buildings in around 10 other towns and cities across other eastern provinces that form the heartland of Ukraine's heavy industry.               \par \par \uc2 In the town of Slaviansk, where the authorities failed to follow through with their announced "anti-terrorist" \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rebels called for Putin's help.               \par \par \uc2 The Kremlin said the Russian president was listening.               \par \par \uc2 "Unfortunately, there's a great many such appeals coming from the eastern Ukrainian regions addressed directly to Putin to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in this or that form," spokesman Dmitry Peskov said. "The president is watching the developments in Eastern Ukraine with great concern."               \par \par \uc2 The Ukraine crisis, and in particular the annexation of Crimea by Russia last month, have led to the most strained relations between Moscow and the West since the Cold War.               \par \par \uc2 The White House has warned Russia it would face further costs over its actions in Ukraine, but it made clear earlier on Monday that the United States was not considering lethal aid for Ukraine.               \par \par \uc2 European foreign ministers agreed to step up sanctions, and U.S. officials have said they were in consultations with European partners on how to punish Moscow for what Kiev and its Western allies call a Russian plot to dismember Ukraine.               \par \par \uc2 Moscow has largely brushed off sanctions so far, which the United States and Europe have explicitly designed to target only a limited number of officials and avert wider economic harm.               \par \par \uc2 OFFENSIVE               \par \par \uc2 Ukraine's interim president, Oleksander Turchinov, said on Monday the offensive against the rebels would still go ahead. But in a sign of discord behind the scenes in Kiev, he sacked the state security chief in charge of the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 .               \par \par \uc2 In one of the first signs of a military deployment by Kiev's forces, a Ukrainian column of two tanks and more than 20 armoured personnel carriers packed with paratroops was seen about 70 km (50 miles) northwest of Slaviansk on Monday evening, according to video journalist Maksim Dondyuk who filmed them.               \par \par \uc2 In Donetsk, rebels holed up in the administrative headquarters of a province that is home to 10 percent of Ukraine's population said they planned to seize control of infrastructure and the levers of state power. They have declared an independent "People's Republic of Donetsk" and sought Putin's protection if they are attacked.               \par \par \uc2 In a bid to undercut the rebels' demands, Turchinov held out the prospect of a countrywide referendum on the future shape of the Ukrainian state. Pro-Russian secessionists want separate referendums in their regions, which Kiev says is illegal.                              \par \par \uc2 'RUSSIAN INVOLVEMENT'               \par \par \uc2 The uprising in eastern Ukraine began eight days ago but has accelerated sharply in the past 48 hours, with separatists seizing ever more buildings, including arsenals filled with weapons. They have met little opposition.               \par \par \uc2 Kiev says the separatists are organised by Moscow, seeking to repeat the seizure of the Crimea region.               \par \par \uc2 Russia says the armed men are all locals acting on their own, but Western officials say the uprising is too well-coordinated to be entirely spontaneous, and bears too many similarities to the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Crimea.               \par \par \uc2 Moscow says it has the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect Russian speakers in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and has portrayed the people of the east as under threat from gangs of Ukrainian-speaking "fascists".               \par \par \uc2 NATO says Russia has tens of thousands of troops massed on the frontier, able to capture eastern Ukraine within days.               \par \par \uc2 "I don't think denials of Russian involvement have a shred of credibility," British Foreign Minister William Hague said, before a meeting with EU counterparts.               \par \par \uc2 Hague later announced that the ministers had agreed to expand a list of Russians barred from travelling or doing business in the EU. Work would begin to come up with new names for the sanctions list, Hague said.               \par \par \uc2 Russian stocks and the rouble fell sharply on Monday, reflecting fears of further Russian military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  and more western sanctions against Moscow.               \par \par \uc2 Kiev is also facing economic disarray. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  nearly doubled its overnight interest rate to 14.50 percent from 7.50 percent. Ukraine's \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost 38 percent of its value against the dollar this year.                              \par \par \uc2 ULTIMATUM EXPIRES               \par \par \uc2 Turchinov had threatened to launch a military crackdown by 9 a.m., but as the deadline expired there was no sign of any action in Slaviansk.               \par \par \uc2 Russia's foreign ministry\uc2  called Turchinov's planned military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  a "criminal order" and said the West should bring its allies in Ukraine's government under control.               \par \par \uc2 Turchinov's website said he told U.N. Secretary-General Ban Ki-moon he would welcome U.N. peacekeepers in Ukraine. The proposal was rhetorical as no such deployment has been proposed or could ever take place over \uc2 Russia's Security Council\uc2  veto.               \par \par \uc2 Turchinov's announcement he was sending in the army was the first time the military has been activated in six months of internal disorder. The plan implies a lack of confidence in the 30,000-strong interior ministry troops, partly discredited by identification with ousted president Viktor Yanukovich.               \par \par \uc2 In Slaviansk, about 150 km (90 miles) from the Russian border, a small airfield that was occupied by Ukrainian air force planes on Sunday was empty on Monday and pro-separatist forces said they were now in control of it.               \par \par \uc2 Eastern Ukraine seems to be rapidly spinning out of the control of the central government. The governor of Donetsk, a multi-millionaire appointed by Kiev, has not been seen since April 11. A man calling himself Donetsk's new police chief has appeared wearing the orange and black separatist ribbon.               \par \par \uc2 The Ukrainian defence ministry acknowledged that it has had difficulty mobilising the armed forces in the east, where some units have been blockaded in by rebellious locals.               \par \par \uc2 "On some occasions we have lost the information war and there have been blockades of our units. People don't understand why they are coming," said acting Defence Minister Mykhailo Koval.               \par \par \uc2 Outside the Slaviansk city council offices stood a group of about 12 armed men in matching camouflage fatigues with black masks, one of whom was holding a Russian flag.               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-4-15T02:39:09.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140414ea4e006y2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
