\par\par \b \uc2 Banks caught in storm as Ukraine tensions rise\b0\par\par\uc2 979 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 19:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Ukraine's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  limits deposits to $1,500 a day\par \par \uc2 * VTB, \uc2 Sberbank\uc2  shares slump\par \par \uc2 * Bankers calm clients, assess risk to deals\par \par \uc2 By \uc2 Megan Davies\par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, March 3 (Reuters) - Bankers scrambled to assess possible damage to corporate deals and tried to calm customers on Monday after Russia's military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  unnerved financial markets and hit bank shares in Russia and across Europe.\par \par \uc2 Both local and foreign banks are likely to be affected if Ukraine's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to fall, loans are not repaid or the country defaults on its sovereign debt even though \uc2 Ukraine's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has imposed limits on the amount depositors can withdraw and is ready to provide liquidity.\par \par \uc2 "Every banker in Ukraine is trying to calm their clients and to feel safe, that's their job," Mykola Chumak, chief executive of Kiev-based private banking advisory firm IDNT, said.\par \par \uc2 "All the clients have got questions about \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates and portfolios and they all want to look into the eyes of their banker and ask the questions directly."\par \par \uc2 Russian's \uc2 Sberbank\uc2 , which had temporarily suspended lending in Ukraine, said it would start lending again when the financial situation there improved.\par \par \uc2 The bank said it had a strong level of liquidity in Ukraine with 1.7 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  available and an unused line of support from its main bank of $750 million.\par \par \uc2 Shares in \uc2 Sberbank\uc2  and VTB, which both have assets in the country, fell 17 percent and 15 percent respectively, a heavier fall than the wider Russian stock indexes .\par \par \uc2 "Banks tend to reflect investor sentiment to Russia more than any other stock so they are being used as a proxy," \uc2 Chris Weafer\uc2 , partner at advisory Macro Advisory, said of the falls in VTB and \uc2 Sberbank\uc2 's shares.\par \par \uc2 Weafer said concern over the rouble weakness was due to the risk it could encourage people to take money out of local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and shrink banks' deposit bases. A weaker rouble also leads to an erosion of consumer confidence which can lead to higher defaults among businesses, Weafer said.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has fallen to a record low while the rouble has dropped 2 percent, although cushioned by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The (Russian banks) cannot pull out (of \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 )," Alexander Danilov, analyst at Fitch, said. "The only thing which they can do - which they've already done - is stop issuing new loans there," he said. "And now they can only sit and wait and try and recover what they have and hope the situation gets back to normal."\par \par \uc2 The crisis could have an impact for investment banks too as they might have to consider pulling upcoming stock market listings due to market turbulence. German retailer Metro's plan to list a stake in its Russian wholesale business is under threat, sources said.\par \par \uc2 Bankers are also trying to assess the impact of the crisis on billions of dollars of Russian loans in the pipeline and whether there will be contagion in the wider syndicated loan market.\par \par \uc2 DEPOSITOR LIMITS\par \par \uc2 For Ukraine's banks, the main risks are a sovereign default or a prolonged slide in the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That could hit their capital levels, Elena Redko, analyst at \uc2 Moody's\uc2  told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 A big part of the banks' \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans are to firms with no with export revenues or foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  revenues, making them susceptible to a fall in the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "So far, the \uc2 National Bank of Ukraine\uc2  has acted proactively to monitor the banking system liquidity," Redko said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already acted to stem potential foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  withdrawals.\par \par \uc2 Local bank Privatbank and the Ukrainian arm of Italy's biggest bank by assets, \uc2 UniCredit\uc2 , also have limits on withdrawals.\par \par \uc2 The largest banks with Ukrainian government debt exposure are state banks \uc2 Savings Bank of Ukraine\uc2 , Ukreximbank and Ukrgazbank plus privately held Delta, according to \uc2 Moody's\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Savings Bank of Ukraine\uc2  said its press service was not accepting calls and did not respond to email. Ukreximbank, Ukrgazbank and Delta Bank did not have working press office numbers and did not respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's new \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  head Stepan Kubiv said last week that the bank was ready to provide liquidity, including cash to banks, according to a statement on the bank's website. "We are in control of the situation in the banking sector," Kubiv said.\par \par \uc2 RUSSIAN EXPOSURE\par \par \uc2 Russian banks, with an estimated $28 billion of assets in Ukraine spread between \uc2 Sberbank\uc2 , VTB, \uc2 Gazprombank\uc2  and \uc2 Vnesheconombank\uc2  (VEB), have a lot at stake.\par \par \uc2 Sberbank\uc2  and VTB have pledged long-term commitment although they are halting lending. VTB has said it has exposure to Ukraine of 20 billion roubles ($560 million), largely through private companies, and that its business there amounts to about 2-3 percent of total \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . VTB's CEO Andrei Kostin said on Wednesday the bank aimed to stay in Ukraine for the long term. On Monday it said it had no further comment on Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Sberbank\uc2 's CEO German Gref said two weeks ago that the bank had no intention of leaving the market. \uc2 Sberbank\uc2  had exposure of 130 billion roubles ($4 billion) to Ukraine - or less than 1 percent of its balance sheet.\par \par \uc2 State development bank VEB said in December its loan exposure in Ukraine was nearly $4 billion, mostly through unit \uc2 Prominvestbank\uc2 . It said last week it had no plans to exit and declined further comment on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Ekaterina Trofimova, a Gazprombank board member, said on Monday the bank was taking all necessary steps in Ukraine to minimise its risks.\par \par \uc2 Other foreign banks in Ukraine have to decide whether to cut losses and go or stay put to grab market share.\par \par \uc2 Raiffeisen Bank International\uc2  said on Monday it has frozen the sale of its Ukrainian arm due to the uncertain situation in the country.\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-3-6T18:54:59.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140303ea3300sux\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
