\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 11-Armed men seize two airports in Ukraine's Crimea, Yanukovich reappears\b0\par\par\uc2 1,554 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2014\par 21:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Ukrainian minister says armed men are Russian forces\par \par \uc2 * Russian fleet denies involvement\par \par \uc2 * Swiss freeze assets of Yanukovich and son\par \par \uc2 * Yanukovich reappears in Russia after week on the run\par \par \uc2 * Ukrainian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pulls out of free fall\par \par \uc2 By Alissa de Carbonnel and Alessandra Prentice\par \par \uc2 SIMFEROPOL, Ukraine, Feb 28 (Reuters) - Armed men took control of two airports in the Crimea region on Friday in what the new Ukrainian leadership described as an invasion by Moscow's forces, and ousted President Viktor Yanukovich surfaced in Russia after a week on the run.\par \par \uc2 Yanukovich said Russia should use all means at its disposal to stop the chaos in Ukraine as tension rose on the Black Sea peninsula of Crimea, the only region with an ethnic Russian majority and the last major bastion of resistance to the overthrow of the Moscow-backed leader.\par \par \uc2 Acting President Oleksander Turchinov accused Russia of open aggression and said Moscow was following a scenario simliar to the one before it went to war with fellow former Soviet republic Georgia in 2008.\par \par \uc2 A day after gunmen seized the Ukrainian parliament and raised the Russian flag, a representative of Turchinov in Crimea said 13 Russian aircraft had landed on the Black Sea peninsula with 150 personnel on board each one.\par \par \uc2 More than 10 Russian military helicopters flew over Crimea and Russian servicemen blockaded a unit of the Ukrainian border guard in the port city of Sevastopol, the guard said.\par \par \uc2 A serviceman at the scene confirmed to Reuters he was from Russia's Black Sea Fleet, part of which is based in Sevastopol, and said they were there to stop the kind of protests that ousted Yanukovich in Kiev.\par \par \uc2 Some witnesses also reported seeing Russian armoured personnel carriers and at least one warship on patrol.\par \par \uc2 The fleet denied its forces were involved in seizing the military airport near Sevastopol, where armed men later also occupied the runway, and a supporter described the armed group at the civilian international airport in Simferopol as Crimean militiamen. Ukraine's commercial airline said later that it had been refused entry into Crimean airspace.\par \par \uc2 Moscow has promised to defend the interests of its citizens in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It has said it will not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  by force, but its rhetoric since the removal of Yanukovich a week ago has echoed the run-up to its invasion of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Any armed confrontation in Crimea would have major global repercussions, with Russia and the West already at odds over the change of power in Ukraine and supporting opposite sides in Syria's civil war. They have, however, pledged to cooperate to prop up Ukraine's faltering economy.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.N. Security Council\uc2  called an emergency session for later on Friday at the request of Ukraine's new leaders, who warned the country's territorial intergrity was threatened. Turchinov said he would not give in to "provocations".\par \par \uc2 KREMLIN ROLE\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's top security official, Andriy Paruby, said the armed men in Crimea were taking their orders from the top in Russia. "These are separate groups ... commanded by the Kremlin," Paruby, secretary of the National Security and Defence Council, told a televised briefing in Kiev.\par \par \uc2 One of the options being considered was declaring a state of emergency in Crimea, he added.\par \par \uc2 The United States warned all parties not to inflame the situation and said it had raised the issue of the reported armed takeovers of the airports with Russia. U.S. officials were seeking clarification of the origin of the armed men.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Secretary of State John Kerry said Moscow, which put 150,000 troops on high alert on Wednesday for war games near Ukraine's border, had told him it had no intention of violating Ukraine's sovereignty.\par \par \uc2 Russian Foreign Minister Sergei Lavrov said Ukraine's new leaders should implement a political deal brokered by the \uc2 European Union\uc2  before Yanukovich's ouster.\par \par \uc2 The Russian Foreign ministry said on its \uc2 Facebook\uc2  page that Russia's Consulate General in Crimea would hand out Russian pasports to the servicemen of Ukraine's now-disbanded Berkut riot police. Protestors had accused the Berkut of firing the live bullets that killed dozens of protesters in Kiev.\par \par \uc2 ALL MEANS\par \par \uc2 Yanukovich - who is wanted by the new, pro-Europe government for mass murder after the protesters' deaths - reappeared in the Russian city of Rostov-on-Don. The new authorities in Kiev started moves to seek his extradition.\par \par \uc2 Yanukovich said he had not seen Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  but had spoken to him by telephone and was surprised the Russian leader was not more vocal on the crisis.\par \par \uc2 "Russia cannot be indifferent, cannot be a bystander watching the fate of as close a partner as Ukraine," Yanukovich told a news conference. "Russia must use all means at its disposal to end the chaos and terror gripping Ukraine."\par \par \uc2 He denied he had run away, saying he had been forced to leave Kiev due to threats and and denounced "lawlessness, terror, anarchy and chaos" in the country and said he had not ordered the shooting of demonstrators that preceeded his fall.\par \par \uc2 Switzerland, Austria and Liechtenstein moved to freeze assets and bank accounts of up to 20 Ukrainians including Yanukovich and his son. Yanukovich said talk of foreign bank accounts was "empty chatter".\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's new rulers have said loans worth $37 billion went missing from state accounts during Yanukovich's three years in power - a jaw-dropping sum even for a population now used to tales of a lavish lifestyle and opulent residence outside Kiev.\par \par \uc2 The new Ukrainian leadership has said the country needs almost as much as that - $35 billion - over the next two years to stave off bankruptcy. It said on Friday it hoped to get financial aid soon and was prepared to fulfil the reform criteria of the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  to get it.\par \par \uc2 IMF chief \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  said she did not see anything on the economic front worthy of panic and urged the leadership to refrain from throwing numbers about she said were meaningless until properly assessed.\par \par \uc2 ARMED INVASION\par \par \uc2 Interior Minister Arsen Avakov accused Russian naval forces of taking over a military airport near the port of Sevastopol, where the Black Sea fleet has a base, and other Russian forces of seizing Simferopol's civilian international airport.\par \par \uc2 "I consider what has happened to be an armed invasion and occupation in violation of all international agreements and norms," Avakov said on his \uc2 Facebook\uc2  page.\par \par \uc2 This met with a Russian naval denial of involvement in the military airport action. "No Black Sea Fleet units have moved toward (the airport), let alone taking any part in blockading it," Interfax quoted a spokesman for the fleet as saying.\par \par \uc2 Near the military airport, half a dozen men in camouflage uniforms with automatic rifles were blocking the road using a truck with no licence plates. Reporters were kept from approaching them by volunteer militia, who formed a second road block about 150 metres away.\par \par \uc2 "Of course they are Russian," said Maxim Lovinetsky, 23, one of the volunteers. "They came last night."\par \par \uc2 Firebrand Russian nationalist Vladimir Zhirinovsky appeared in Sevastopol where a crowd outside the city administration gave him a hero's welcome, shouting "Russia, thank you!".\par \par \uc2 "If the people have a right to rise up in a revolt and overthrow the authorities, why doesn't Sevastopol have a right to do that?" he told them. Although nominally part of the Russian opposition, he is widely seen as a servant of Kremlin policy, used to float radical opinions to test public reaction.\par \par \uc2 AVOIDING PROVOCATIONS\par \par \uc2 The United States has told Russia to show in the next few days that it is sincere about a promise not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying using force would be a grave mistake.\par \par \uc2 The Kremlin said Putin had ordered his government to continue talks with Ukraine on economic and trade relations and to consult foreign partners including the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  on financial aid.\par \par \uc2 Yanukovich provoked protests in Ukraine in November by backing out of plans to sign landmark deals with the \uc2 European Union\uc2  and instead saying Kiev would seek closer economic and trade ties with its former Soviet master Russia.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose on Friday from historic lows after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor limited access to foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading around 9.80-10.10 to the dollar after weakening as far as 11.20-10.10 on Thursday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been in freefall as investors worried about Kiev's ability to repay its debts.\par \par \uc2 Kiev's new rulers have said any movement by Russian forces beyond the base in Sevastopol would be tantamount to aggression. But they face a major challenge in Crimea which was Russian territory until it was transferred to Ukraine in 1954, during the Soviet era. Separatism there has often flared up at times of tension between Moscow and Kiev.\par \par \uc2 Armed men took control of Simferopol airport overnight and a Reuters eyewitness said the men, dressed in full battle gear and carrying assault rifles and machine guns, were moving freely in and out of the control tower.\par \par \uc2 A man called Vladimir, who said he was a volunteer helping the group, said: "We're simple people, volunteers ... We're here at the airport to maintain order."\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-3-3T20:59:49.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140228ea2s00b9x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
