\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar slides after Yellen comments; yen and Swiss franc rise\b0\par\par\uc2 628 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Februar 2014\par 22:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Dollar index falls as Yellen remarks suggest dovish bent\par \par \uc2 * Ruble recovers losses as Ukraine news turns more positive\par \par \uc2 * Yen, Swiss franc gain on worries over emerging markets\par \par \uc2 By Sam Forgione\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Feb 27 (Reuters) - The dollar fell on Thursday against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after comments by Federal Reserve Chair \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  suggested the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could maintain its easy monetary policies for longer than expected.\par \par \uc2 Yellen, in comments before the Senate Banking Committee, failed to provide clarity on the impact of a harsh winter on recent economic weakness and reiterated concerns about the state of the U.S. labor market.\par \par \uc2 "The dollar reversed it's earlier bullish course and moved back lower after traders interpreted Yellen's comments as being a bit more dovish," said \uc2 Greg Michalowski\uc2 , chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at FXDD in Phoenix.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the Fed could delay reductions to its monthly bond-buying program and keep interest rates low for longer if it attributed recent weak U.S. economic data to a broader economic slowdown rather than cold weather.\par \par \uc2 An expectation of prolonged easy-money policies hurts the dollar because lower interest rates deter capital flows into the United States.\par \par \uc2 The yen and the Swiss franc rose on jitters over the possibility of Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 , adding to concerns about emerging markets and driving capital into the world's traditional safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Russian ruble hit a five-year low against the dollar on Thursday, as tensions escalated in Ukraine after Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  ordered drills by his armed forces to test combat readiness in western Russia, near the border with Ukraine. The dollar rose against the ruble to 36.2670, the highest level since February 2009, but then pared gains to trade mostly flat against the ruble on more positive news on Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The greenback last traded against the ruble at 36.0125. The \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  said it would send a fact-finding team to Ukraine next week in response to Kiev's request for support after the ouster of President Viktor Yanukovich, which helped the ruble recover losses.\par \par \uc2 "It's a little bit of a turn in sentiment," said Win Thin, global head of emerging markets strategy at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 Even so, in another sign of the tensions in Russia and Ukraine, the dollar was last up 5 percent against Ukraine's \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , hitting a record high of 10.6 \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The dollar index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was last down 0.14 percent at 80.313, a day after holding near a two-week high.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was down against the euro, which last traded up 0.14 percent at $1.3706. The dollar was down 0.25 percent against the yen, at 102.09, and was last down 0.23 percent against the Swiss franc, which traded at 0.88855.\par \par \uc2 Traders largely shrugged off Labor Department data showing initial claims for U.S. unemployment benefits increased 14,000 last week to a seasonally adjusted 348,000.\par \par \uc2 Other reports showed orders for long-lasting U.S. manufactured goods excluding transportation unexpectedly rose last month, as did a gauge of business spending plans. Both had little effect on the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The dollar last traded mostly flat against the Chinese yuan, which has had its most severe fall in years. Traders have said that the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  has engineered the drop, but that it also reflects worries that the Chinese economy, which has propped up global growth for a decade, is stuttering.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was last at 6.1284 versus the yuan. \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-2-27T22:23:01.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140227ea2r00etd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
