\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar near 2-week high as geopolitical tensions heat up\b0\par\par\uc2 523 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Februar 2014\par 04:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * U.S. dollar stays near 2-week highs hit the previous day               \par \par \uc2 * U.S. warns Russia it would be 'grave mistake' to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  militarily in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Aussie dollar slips on weak domestic capital spending data               \par \par \uc2 By Ian Chua and Masayuki Kitano               \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY/SINGAPORE, Feb 27 (Reuters) - The U.S. dollar held near a two-week high against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday, as heightening tensions in Ukraine helped support safe-haven demand for the greenback.               \par \par \uc2 The dollar index was steady at 80.405, not very far from Wednesday's peak of 80.524, its highest since Feb. 13.               \par \par \uc2 The dollar was underpinned after emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2   came under renewed pressure on Wednesday, led by a fall in Ukraine's \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  abandoned a managed exchange rate policy in favour of a flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .               \par \par \uc2 Raising tensions in the region, President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  ordered 150,000 Russian troops to be ready for war games near Ukraine, prompting the United States to warn Russia that it would be a "grave mistake" to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  militarily.               \par \par \uc2 Protest leaders in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a former Soviet republic that Putin has called a "brother nation", want to form a new government following the overthrow of pro-Russia President Viktor Yanukovich.               \par \par \uc2 Against the dollar, the euro held steady at $1.3687. On Wednesday, the euro had touched a low of about $1.3662, its lowest level in about two weeks.               \par \par \uc2 The heightened tensions in Ukraine could keep the euro on the defensive, analysts said.               \par \par \uc2 "Clearly it's all very sensitive at the moment and very, very tenuous I guess in terms of the situation," said Mitul Kotecha, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy for \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2  in Hong Kong.               \par \par \uc2 "I think that's probably what could provoke a bigger impact, because obviously you could end up seeing a big escalation of tensions between Russia and the West," he added.               \par \par \uc2 The euro could prove particularly vulnerable versus the yen if tensions grow and risk aversion increases, Kotecha added.               \par \par \uc2 The euro's moves versus the yen in Asia on Thursday were subdued, however, with the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  holding steady at 140.12 yen.               \par \par \uc2 The dollar edged up 0.1 percent versus the yen to 102.41 yen .               \par \par \uc2 To be sure, there was no widespread panic in markets despite the heightened geopolitical risk. Asian equities were little changed on Thursday, while Wall Street ended nearly flat on Wednesday.               \par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar fell 0.4 percent to $0.8935, coming under pressure after data showed a surprise 5.2 percent fall in Australian capital spending in the fourth quarter, the biggest drop in more than four years.               \par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve Chair \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 's weather-delayed testimony before the Senate Banking Committee will be closely watched by the market later on Thursday.               \par \par \uc2 "It would be a huge surprise if she amended the prepared testimony, but there should be plenty of interest in the lengthy Q&A session in front of the Senate Banking Committee," said Sean Callow, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Westpac\uc2  in Sydney.               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-2-27T04:53:11.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140227ea2r003s5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
