\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Asian shares soft as Ukraine dampens risk sentiment\b0\par\par\uc2 849 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Februar 2014\par 07:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Russian rouble hits 5-year low vs dollar\par \par \uc2 * Dollar index at 2-week high, U.S. yields at 3-week low\par \par \uc2 * Yuan rebounds but still below \uc2 PBOC\uc2  fixing\par \par \uc2 * European shares seen falling slightly\par \par \uc2 By Hideyuki Sano\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 27 (Reuters) - Asian shares struggled to find a solid footing on Thursday as escalating tensions in Ukraine sent investors scurrying to the safety of the dollar and U.S. Treasuries.\par \par \uc2 Japanese stocks skidded, with the Nikkei slipping 0.4 percent although MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan managed to erase early losses to eke out small gains on a rebound in Chinese shares.\par \par \uc2 European shares are expected to fall slightly, with Germany's DAX and Britain's FTSE seen falling about 0.1 percent.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street's failure to extend its rally above historical highs on Wednesday did not help soothe fears that a wider conflagration in Ukraine could intensify risk aversion.\par \par \uc2 Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  ordered drills by his armed forces to test combat readiness in western Russia, near the border with \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 , prompting Washington to warn a military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be a "grave mistake."\par \par \uc2 "Clearly it's all very sensitive at the moment," said Mitul Kotecha, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy for \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2  in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 "I think that's probably what could provoke a bigger impact, because obviously you could end up seeing a big escalation of tensions between Russia and the West," he added.\par \par \uc2 The Russian rouble tumbled to a five-year low against the dollar and stayed near an all time-low against the euro hit on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainian \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit record lows on Wednesday after \uc2 Ukraine's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it was abandoning a managed exchange rate policy.\par \par \uc2 Safe-haven U.S. Treasuries benefited from the sombre mood in markets, with the 10-year U.S debt yield falling to a three-week low of 2.662 percent, despite surprise strength in U.S. new home sales data.\par \par \uc2 Investors are now looking to comments from Fed chief \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 's testimony at a \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  committee on Thursday on her views on a recent run of soft U.S. data, which investors chalk up to bad weather, rather than weakening in the fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 If that is the case, investors are likely to expect the Fed to keep trimming its bond purchase by $10 billion at each policy meeting to end it completely by the end of this year.\par \par \uc2 The dollar also strengthened broadly, with the dollar index hitting its highest level in about two weeks.\par \par \uc2 "Given U.S. debt yields fell and that U.S. shares were steady to softer, the dollar's strength should be regarded as a reflection of risk aversion rather than rising confidence in the U.S. economy," said \uc2 Masafumi Yamamoto\uc2 , chief strategist at Praevidentia Strategy.\par \par \uc2 The euro traded at $1.3681, after having fallen 0.4 percent to two-week lows on Wednesday. Against the yen, which also tends to rise when markets are under stress, the dollar was little changed at 102.37 yen.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese yuan remained pressured near a seven-month low hit on Wednesday, trading below the daily fixing set by Beijing for a third consecutive day.\par \par \uc2 The yuan stood at 6.1300 per dollar, near Wednesday's low of 6.1351 and below the fixing at 6.1224.\par \par \uc2 Traders suspect the \uc2 People's Bank of China (PBOC) \uc2 is possibly aiming to inject more two-way volatility into the market and prepare it for more reforms.\par \par \uc2 "It's possible that the Chinese authorities think they need a weaker yuan now to bolster the economy," said Hirokazu Yuihama, senior strategist at Daiwa Securities.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong shares rose, led by state oil giant Sinopec but the CSI300 of the biggest Shanghai and Shenzhen A-share listings, under pressure from concerns over slowdown as well as fear of lending curbs on property developers, slipped to near an eight-month low set on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Most other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  held steady for now but the tense backdrop could put renewed pressure on emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and shares. They were battered earlier this year by concerns about slower global growth and the tapering of the U.S. Federal Reserve's monetary stimulus.\par \par \uc2 Turkey, one of the hardest-hit countries, saw its lira slipping to three-week lows, dented also by a corruption scandal embroiling Prime Minister Tayyip Erdogan's government.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its benchmark interest rate on Wednesday to 10.75 percent from 10.50 percent as expected, slowing the pace of monetary tightening to avoid hurting an economy that is flirting with recession.\par \par \uc2 Copper dropped to a three-month low below $7,000 a tonne, extending its losses over the past week on concerns about slower growth in China.\par \par \uc2 Gold also stepped back after hitting a four-month high on Wednesday as the dollar strengthened broadly. It last stood at $1,325.90 an ounce, off Wednesday's high of $1,345.35. \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-2-27T07:49:07.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140227ea2r0005t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex