\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Markets Seeking Cues; Ukraine At Back Of Investors' Minds -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,513 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Februar 2014\par 22:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2112 GMT [Dow Jones] Asian markets will be looking for trading cues with U.S. markets relatively flat. Grant Williamson, a broker at Christchurch-based Hamilton Hindin Greene, says concerns about Ukraine seemed to be having little impact on U.S. markets and therefore are unlikely to weigh on Asian markets, "although it would be in the back of investors' minds." Locally, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Air New Zealand\f28 \uc2 \f2  (AIR.NZ) investors will be pleased with its record interim profit "which is an absolute cracker," Williamson says, while news Fonterra is lifting its milk payout forecast is good for local economy's outlook. The NZX-50 is flat at 4975.00. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com, @lucy_craymer)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2057 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD is lower as concerns about the situation in Ukraine and headlines reporting increased posturing by Russia gave the USD a broad lift, says ANZ in a morning note. January trade and migration data will drive NZD direction today, it says. The median in a Wall Street Journal poll of 10 economists is for a trade surplus of NZ$221 million in January, and a surplus of NZ$235 million for the 12 months to January. The pair is at 0.8307 vs 0.8329 late Wednesday and is expected to trade in a short-term range of 0.8240-0.8360. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com, @lucy_craymer)\par \par \uc2 15:42 EST - Many analysts focused on the disappointing headline number of 1.4% overall growth in StatsCan's report on investment plans for 2014, but National Bank Financial says the details are more encouraging. Focusing on private-sector investment in the machinery and equipment, and intellectual property areas, investments are expected to grow 3.3% this year, NBF says. That spending will increase in each of the 10 provinces except Saskatchewan, where it was at a record level last year. "It is clear that Corporate Canada intends to increase the pace of investments susceptible to enhance productivity," NBF says. (don.curren@wsj.com; @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 14:53 EST - Russia's ruble and Ukraine's \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold off on news about Russia testing its military's readiness. "This raises fear that Russia might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says Jason Rogan, managing director of \uc2 US government\uc2  bond trading at Guggenheim Securities. "If it does, who knows where this would lead -- It is the uncertainty that drove the flight-to-safety bid in Treasury bonds." The market had largely brushed off the fluid situation in Ukraine over the past week, with only news of Russia's actions today striking the first nerve. Treasurys rounding out the day at session highs, with 10-year notes up 10/32 to yield 2.667%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 13:47 EST - The strong five-year note sale is a sign some investors are unwinding short bets, traders say. Shorts on the five-year notes rose earlier this year as investors bet shorter-dated bonds would be vulnerable to rising yields this year. But mixed growth data have sent bond yields lower, pushing some investors to buy bonds to cover bearish bets. For now, expectations that stronger growth would force the Fed to raise short-term rates sooner than late 2015 hasn't panned out, putting shorts on five-year notes under water. Five-year notes up 2/32 to yield 1.498%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 13:15 EST - Bids poured into the US's $35B five-year note offering, enough to cover the auction 2.98 times. That's the highest at a sale of this maturity since September 2012. Indirect bidders, often a reflection of foreign demand, took more than half the sale, the most for this group since July. The eager demand there left direct bidders with just 9.2%, the lowest since July. Looks like the 1.53% yield offered was enough to satisfy buyers despite recent jitters about the Fed hiking rates sooner than expected. Middle-maturity Treasurys continue to be most at risk in the current environment, since they are at the center of Fed QE purchases. Treasury prices rise after the strong sale, with 10-year notes up 3/32 to yield 2.692%. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 12:59 EST - \uc2 Banco de Credito del Peru\uc2  thinks the country's economy will show strong recovery signs starting in 2H after a 2013 slowdown. Peru's biggest bank sees improvement on increased exports "of traditional products, a sustained recovery in business and consumer expectations and dynamism of public investments in a context of regional and local elections." Banco de Credito expects full-year growth of 5.5% in 2014, versus last year's 5%, the least in 4 years. (ryan.dube@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:28 EST - Flow data in the BLS employment report track a specific subset of the American workforce: those not participating in the labor force one month but end up with a job the next. Out of the 809K who weren't in the labor force in December but entered in January, a record 747K did so with jobs. It's unclear why and if it'll happen again in February, but \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  suspects the expiration of extended jobless benefits has something to do with it--perhaps compelling inactive family members of those who lost benefits to take jobs. The investment bank says that incentive could add to the payrolls count in coming months. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 1724 GMT [Dow Jones] The RUB tumbled to a record low (42.1) versus the EUR-USD basket Wednesday, as the political situation between Ukraine and Russia worsened. "The current troubles at the moment don't help" the RUB says Peter Kinsella, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  in London. Russia is suffering continuing capital flight as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the Russian Federation\uc2  moves towards a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime, notes Mr Kinsella. "The fundamentals in Russia are poor and are worsening - and that's why you're going to see a consistently weaker RUB in the weeks and months ahead," adds Mr Kinsella. (clare.connaghan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:15 EST - The young $28B market of \uc2 US government\uc2  floating-rate notes is still in the process of finding price levels where sellers best match buyers. After its long-awaited debut last month saw buyers clamor for the inaugural auction, allowing the government to pay a small 4.5 bps spread, the spread has since trended higher to the 6-7-bp area. Overseas government accounts, which are less price-sensitive and may want the new notes for diversification purposes, have been strong buyers as reflected in the eager indirect bids. As they get their fill, watch for the yield spread to widen toward 10 bps, some analysts say, as domestic money funds demand more compensation for switching to 2-year maturities. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 11:50 EST - \uc2 US government\uc2 's second-ever floating-rate Treasury sale drew a solid turnout, attracting 5.29 times the bid necessary to cover the $13B offering. Indirect bidders continue to show strong interest, taking nearly 40% of the total sale, vs last month's 37.8%. Direct bidders took 5.7% from 8.9%. The 6.4 bps spread demanded on the floaters (the extra yield buyers get on top of the 0.045% three-month bill yield) is more than the 4.5 bps spread in January, but still reflects strong demand because it's less than the going market rate at the time of the sale. Next up: the Treasury sells $35B in five-year notes at 1:00pm. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)(john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 11:49 EST - The new type of \uc2 US government\uc2  bond sale continues to attract decent demand in its second auction. The bid to cover for the $13B sale of two-year floating-rate notes was 5.29. Indirect bid, a proxy of foreign demand, was 39.7% while direct bid was 5.7%. The government paid a higher interest rate compared to the sale in January--0.064% spread plus the auctioned yield of this week's three-month Tbills, up from 0.045% from the January sale. Fitch says in a report earlier this month that bidding from money funds in January has been cautious but it believes demand could increase in coming months as more sales expand liquidity. Treasury aims to broaden the investor base to lure money funds, corporate treasurers and central banks, typically Tbill buyers. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 26, 2014 16:12 ET (21:12 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140226ea2q003y2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
