\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Ukraine's trade gap doubles in 2011\b0\par\par\uc2 396 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Februar 2012\par 11:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Falling demand for steel, rising gas price put pressure on trade balance\par \par \uc2 * Govt is pessimistic on external environment this year\par \par \uc2 * Cbank keep burning reserves to maintain \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg\par \par \uc2 (Adds data on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January)\par \par \uc2 KIEV, Feb 15 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's trade deficit more than doubled in 2011 from the previous year as a global slowdown weighed on steel exports and the government warned foreign demand for its raw materials and grain was likely to be weaker in the coming year.\par \par \uc2 Imports of goods and services exceeded exports by $6.748 billion last year, up from $3.025 billion in 2010, the State Statistics Service said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Demand declined for Ukraine's key export, steel, towards the end of 2011, while higher prices for Russian gas, on which the country relies for its energy needs, has also put pressure on the trade balance.\par \par \uc2 The data showed exports grew by 33 percent in 2011 to $68.410 billion, outrun by imports which increased by 36 percent to $82.607 billion. Ukraine's trade-in-goods deficit - which includes steel - widened to $14.197 billion in 2011 from $9.337 billion in the previous year.\par \par \uc2 The poor global economic climate means the government expects the Ukrainian economy to grow to 3.9 percent growth this year from 5.2 percent in 2011.\par \par \uc2 "Europe will not resolve its debt crisis quickly and efficiently now. Asian markets are slowing down," Prime Minister Mykola Azarov told a government meeting on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "We should expect neither (getting) external financial resources, nor favourable conditions for traditional Ukrainian exports."\par \par \uc2 The trade deficit puts pressure on Ukraine's \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  Ukraine's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has spent about $7 billion, nearly a fifth of its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, since last September to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pegged at around 8 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 In January alone, the bank's net foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales reached $894 million, it said on Wednesday. The bank's reserves stood at $31.4 billion at the end of January.\par \par \uc2 Kiev concluded talks on a landmark trade pact with the European Union in October, but its implementation remains stalled by a dispute over the jailing of opposition leader Yulia Tymoshenko, which Brussels says was politically motivated.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Olzhas Auyezov and Natalya Zinets; editing by Patrick Graham)\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE-TRADE/  (UPDATE 2)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120215e82f000gk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
